        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: How Breaker Finders Actually Work -->
        <div id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Breaker Finders Actually Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Understanding the mechanism explains both why these tools sometimes indicate on several breakers and what you can do about it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Transmitter Modulates the Circuit</h3>

          <p>
            Plugged into an outlet, the transmitter draws a rapidly pulsed load, or in some designs injects a distinct signal onto the conductors. Either way it creates a distinctive pattern of current that travels back along the circuit to the panel.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Receiver Detects a Magnetic Field</h3>

          <p>
            Current flowing in a wire produces a magnetic field around it. The receiver is an inductive pickup tuned to the transmitter's pattern, so passing it over the breakers detects the field around the conductor carrying that specific signal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Adjacent Conductors Pick Up the Signal Too</h3>

          <p>
            Wires running together in a panel and along the same route through the building couple with each other, so the signal appears weakly on neighbours. That is why a finder set to high sensitivity indicates on several breakers, and why reducing sensitivity is the technique that isolates one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Circuit Must Usually Be Live</h3>

          <p>
            Most transmitters draw their power from the circuit they are plugged into, which means a dead circuit produces no signal. Tone generators and professional locators inject their own signal and work on de-energised conductors, which is the main functional difference between the categories.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Confirmation Still Belongs to a Voltage Tester</h3>

          <p>
            A breaker finder tells you which breaker to switch off. It does not tell you that the circuit is dead. Verifying that with a voltage tester at the outlet, before touching anything, is the step that keeps the tool a convenience rather than a hazard.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
