        <!-- SECTION 2: What to Look For When Choosing -->
        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What to Look For When Choosing
          </h2>

          <p>
            The specifications that matter are not the ones usually advertised, and a few features change the experience considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sensitivity control is the most important feature.</strong> Whether automatic or manual, the ability to step sensitivity down is what isolates one breaker from its neighbours. A tool with a fixed sensitivity will always indicate on a group.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Numeric signal strength beats a simple beep.</strong> On a crowded panel where several breakers register, a number tells you which registers most strongly. A binary indicator leaves you guessing between three candidates.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A GFCI test function is worth having.</strong> It costs the manufacturer very little to include and it means you can verify GFCI protection while already at the outlet, which is a job most people never get around to otherwise.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Receiver size matters more than you expect.</strong> Sweeping a receiver across breakers inside a crowded panel with a dead front removed is fiddly, and a bulky receiver makes it worse. Compact tools are genuinely easier to use in the situations that are already difficult.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check whether it works through a closed panel door.</strong> Some finders detect strongly enough to work with the door shut, which is faster and safer since it avoids removing the dead front at all. Others require direct access to each breaker.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Consider whether you need de-energised tracing.</strong> If your use case is renovation work with dead circuits, an ordinary finder is the wrong tool entirely and you need a tone generator or a professional locator.
          </p>
        </div>

