        <!-- SECTION 4: Getting a Clean Result -->
        <div id="using-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Getting a Clean Result
          </h2>

          <p>
            Technique matters as much as the tool, and a few habits turn an ambiguous result into a definite one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Start at maximum sensitivity, then reduce.</strong> The first sweep confirms the signal is reaching the panel at all and shows the general area. Each subsequent sweep at lower sensitivity eliminates the weakly coupled neighbours until one breaker remains.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sweep at a consistent speed and distance.</strong> Holding the receiver at a varying distance from the breakers changes the reading for reasons that have nothing to do with which circuit you are on. Rest it lightly and move steadily.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sweep the whole panel before deciding.</strong> Stopping at the first indication is how people label the wrong breaker. Continue to the end, note every breaker that registers, then reduce sensitivity and repeat.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Watch for shared neutrals on multi-wire circuits.</strong> Two circuits sharing a neutral conductor will both carry part of the signal, which produces two genuine indications. In older houses this is common and it is not a tool fault.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Turn off large loads first.</strong> A running air conditioner, a heat pump, or a heavily loaded circuit generates magnetic fields that swamp a weak signal. Switching off the biggest loads before starting makes a real difference in a busy panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Verify by switching it off.</strong> Once you believe you have the right breaker, switch it and check the outlet with a voltage tester. That takes ten seconds and it is the only step that turns a guess into knowledge.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Label the Panel While You Are There</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Once you have identified a circuit, write it on the panel directory immediately. Almost nobody does this, which is why the same identification job gets repeated every few years in every house. An afternoon spent mapping and labelling every circuit is the highest value use of a breaker finder you will ever get.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: When the Finder Struggles -->
        <div id="difficult" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When the Finder Struggles
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some installations defeat these tools, and knowing which ones saves a great deal of frustration blaming the equipment.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sub panels are harder than main panels.</strong> The signal travels further and passes through more shared conductors, and by the time it reaches the sub panel the difference between the target breaker and its neighbours is small. Premium tools with fine sensitivity control do far better here.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Metal conduit shields the signal.</strong> Circuits run in metal conduit or armoured cable produce a much weaker external magnetic field, which can reduce a strong signal to something barely detectable. Increasing sensitivity helps, at the cost of more false indications.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Very long runs attenuate the signal.</strong> A circuit reaching a detached garage or the far corner of a large house delivers less signal to the panel, and cheap finders may not detect it at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>AFCI and GFCI breakers can interfere.</strong> The electronics inside these breakers change how the signal presents, and some finders react unpredictably. This is not a defect so much as an interaction, and it varies by combination of tool and breaker.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Aluminium wiring behaves differently.</strong> Older aluminium branch circuits couple differently and sometimes give weaker or less distinct readings. It is worth a slower, more careful sweep rather than assuming the tool has failed.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The old method still works.</strong> When a finder gives an ambiguous answer, switching candidate breakers off one at a time and checking the outlet resolves it definitively. Use the finder to reduce forty candidates to three, then confirm the old way.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Mapping and Labelling a Whole Panel -->
        <div id="mapping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Mapping and Labelling a Whole Panel
          </h2>

          <p>
            The highest value job a breaker finder does is not identifying one circuit in an emergency. It is spending an afternoon mapping every circuit in the house once, so nobody ever has to guess again.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Work outlet by outlet, not breaker by breaker.</strong> Starting at the panel and switching breakers off means walking the house repeatedly for each one. Starting at the outlets and using the finder means one trip to the panel per circuit, which is dramatically faster and does not interrupt anything running.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Write on paper first, the directory second.</strong> A rough sketch of the house with circuit numbers pencilled onto each room lets you correct mistakes as you go. Transferring a clean version onto the panel directory at the end produces something legible rather than something crossed out.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Include what is on each circuit, not just the room.</strong> A label reading kitchen is far less useful than one reading kitchen counter outlets, north wall. The person reading it in five years will be trying to isolate one specific thing, and room names rarely narrow it down.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not forget the hardwired loads.</strong> Lights, the furnace, the dishwasher, the disposal, the garage door opener and the bathroom fan all have breakers and none of them has an outlet to plug a transmitter into. Identify these by switching breakers and observing, and note them.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Photograph the finished directory.</strong> A picture on a phone means the information survives a directory card going missing, a panel being replaced, or the label fading. It also means you can check from anywhere in the house rather than walking back.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Note anything that surprises you.</strong> A bathroom outlet on the same breaker as a bedroom, or a garage circuit reaching into the kitchen, is worth recording and possibly worth mentioning to an electrician. Odd groupings often indicate work done at different times without a plan.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Safety Around a Live Panel -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Safety Around a Live Panel
          </h2>

          <p>
            Using a breaker finder means opening an electrical panel, which deserves more caution than the simplicity of the tool suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Removing the dead front exposes live parts.</strong> Behind the cover, the main lugs and busbars are energised and remain so with every breaker switched off. Those are the parts that cause serious injuries, and they are close to where you will be sweeping a receiver.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Work with one hand where you can.</strong> Keeping the other hand out of contact with anything grounded reduces the chance of current crossing your chest if something goes wrong. It is a habit worth having before you need it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use insulated tools and dry conditions.</strong> Standing on a damp basement floor while reaching into a panel is a bad combination. Dry footwear, a dry surface, and adequate light are minimal precautions.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never work in a wet or corroded panel.</strong> Rust streaks, water staining, or a burning smell mean the panel needs an electrician rather than a breaker finder. These are signs of a problem that identification will not solve.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Older panels of certain brands deserve extra caution.</strong> Some panel types from decades past have known safety issues and should be evaluated by an electrician rather than worked on casually. If yours is one of them, that assessment is overdue anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Know your limits.</strong> Identifying a breaker is a reasonable homeowner task. Anything requiring conductors to be moved, terminals to be loosened, or a panel to be modified is electrician work, and the cost of getting it wrong is not proportional to the money saved.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best circuit breaker finder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Klein Tools ET450 for most people, because its automatic sensitivity adjustment narrows down to a single breaker more reliably than manual tools. For crowded panels the Ideal 61-534 gives a numeric signal strength that resolves ambiguity, and Sperry CS61200 is the sensible budget choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How accurate are circuit breaker finders?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a tidy modern panel, a good finder identifies the correct breaker first time. On a crowded old panel or a sub panel, expect several breakers to register and to need two or three passes at reducing sensitivity. Always confirm by switching the breaker and testing the outlet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do circuit breaker finders work on dead circuits?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most do not. The transmitter draws power from the circuit it is plugged into, so a de-energised circuit produces no signal. Tone generators like the Triplett Fox and Hound, and professional locators like the Fluke 2042, inject their own signal and work on dead conductors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my breaker finder indicate on several breakers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Conductors running together in a panel couple magnetically, so the signal appears weakly on neighbouring circuits. Reducing sensitivity progressively eliminates the weak indications and leaves the strong one. Shared neutrals on multi-wire circuits also produce two genuinely correct indications.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a breaker finder on a sub panel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but less reliably. The signal has travelled further and through more shared conductors, so the difference between the target breaker and its neighbours is smaller. Tools with fine sensitivity control or a numeric readout handle this much better than simple budget finders.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to use a circuit breaker finder myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Using the transmitter at an outlet is straightforward. Sweeping the receiver requires opening the panel, and behind the dead front the main lugs and busbars stay energised even with every breaker off. Work in dry conditions with good light, keep one hand clear, and stop if the panel shows rust, water staining or burning smells.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Breaker Finding Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most breaker finder frustration comes down to technique rather than the tool, and a few corrections fix nearly all of it.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stopping at the First Indication</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sweeping down the panel and labelling the first breaker that lights up is how the wrong circuit gets marked. Several breakers commonly register on the first pass because adjacent conductors couple magnetically. Sweep the entire panel, note everything that registers, then reduce sensitivity and repeat until one remains.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Reducing Sensitivity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Maximum sensitivity finds the signal and also finds every neighbour that has picked it up. The whole technique is progressive reduction, and a tool used at one setting throughout will always give an ambiguous answer. Automatic tools do this for you; manual tools require you to do it deliberately.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Tool Instead of a Voltage Tester</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A breaker finder tells you which breaker to switch off. It does not tell you the circuit is dead, and treating it as though it does is how people work on live conductors. Switch the breaker, then verify at the outlet with a voltage tester before touching anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Labelling the Panel Afterwards</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Having done the work of identifying a circuit, almost nobody writes it down, which is why the same job gets repeated every few years. Mapping and labelling every circuit in an afternoon is by far the most valuable thing a breaker finder ever does for a household.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

